    "question": {
      ":question/id": "36dbb583-7caf-53c9-adff-fed149461c23",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $y=2 + 3 e^{- x}$\n- **II.** $y=-2 - e^{- x}$\n- **III.** $y=e^{x} - 2$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the stated domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=2 + 3 e^{- x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- 3 e^{- x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ - 3 e^{- x} & \\overset{?}{=}1\\cdot \\left(2 + 3 e^{- x}\\right) \\\\ - 3 e^{- x} & \\overset{?}{=}2 + 3 e^{- x} \\\\ -2 - 6 e^{- x} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=2 + 3 e^{- x}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-2 - e^{- x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=e^{- x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ e^{- x} & \\overset{?}{=}1\\cdot \\left(-2 - e^{- x}\\right) \\\\ e^{- x} & \\overset{?}{=}-2 - e^{- x} \\\\ 2 + 2 e^{- x} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=-2 - e^{- x}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=e^{x} - 2,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=e^{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ e^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}1\\cdot \\left(e^{x} - 2\\right) \\\\ e^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}e^{x} - 2 \\\\ 2 & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=e^{x} - 2$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
