  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186100959,
      "id": "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc",
      "title": "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "f7bfc7d8-1c1b-5646-8a42-ac5cde643918",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $y=3 e^{x} + 1$\n- **II.** $y=2 - e^{x}$\n- **III.** $y=2 - 2 e^{x}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the stated domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=3 e^{x} + 1,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3 e^{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ 3 e^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}1\\cdot \\left(3 e^{x} + 1\\right) \\\\ 3 e^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}3 e^{x} + 1 \\\\ -1 & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=3 e^{x} + 1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=2 - e^{x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- e^{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ - e^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}1\\cdot \\left(2 - e^{x}\\right) \\\\ - e^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}2 - e^{x} \\\\ -2 & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=2 - e^{x}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=2 - 2 e^{x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- 2 e^{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ - 2 e^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}1\\cdot \\left(2 - 2 e^{x}\\right) \\\\ - 2 e^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}2 - 2 e^{x} \\\\ -2 & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=2 - 2 e^{x}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "ef675dbb-01aa-5ee8-847a-f897652727b8",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
