  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186100959,
      "id": "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc",
      "title": "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "fd33aa0d-f9a2-5011-8922-300093f436f8",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- y.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $y=- 3 e^{x} - 1$\n- **II.** $y=1 + 3 e^{- x}$\n- **III.** $y=3 e^{x} + 2$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the stated domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=- 3 e^{x} - 1,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- 3 e^{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =- y \\\\ - 3 e^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}-1\\cdot \\left(- 3 e^{x} - 1\\right) \\\\ - 3 e^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}3 e^{x} + 1 \\\\ - 6 e^{x} - 1 & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=- 3 e^{x} - 1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=1 + 3 e^{- x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- 3 e^{- x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =- y \\\\ - 3 e^{- x} & \\overset{?}{=}-1\\cdot \\left(1 + 3 e^{- x}\\right) \\\\ - 3 e^{- x} & \\overset{?}{=}-1 - 3 e^{- x} \\\\ 1 & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=1 + 3 e^{- x}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=3 e^{x} + 2,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3 e^{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =- y \\\\ 3 e^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}-1\\cdot \\left(3 e^{x} + 2\\right) \\\\ 3 e^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}- 3 e^{x} - 2 \\\\ 6 e^{x} + 2 & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=3 e^{x} + 2$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "8292c94c-00e4-5e30-892f-cdb9c74c4e58",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
