      ":question/id": "2d8e9380-ede2-5a00-80ef-444b23c31efb",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- y.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $y=- 3 e^{x}$\n- **II.** $y=e^{x} - 1$\n- **III.** $y=3 e^{- x}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the stated domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=- 3 e^{x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- 3 e^{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =- y \\\\ - 3 e^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}-1\\cdot \\left(- 3 e^{x}\\right) \\\\ - 3 e^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}3 e^{x} \\\\ - 6 e^{x} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=- 3 e^{x}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=e^{x} - 1,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=e^{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =- y \\\\ e^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}-1\\cdot \\left(e^{x} - 1\\right) \\\\ e^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}1 - e^{x} \\\\ 2 e^{x} - 1 & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=e^{x} - 1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=3 e^{- x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- 3 e^{- x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =- y \\\\ - 3 e^{- x} & \\overset{?}{=}-1\\cdot \\left(3 e^{- x}\\right) \\\\ - 3 e^{- x} & =- 3 e^{- x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=3 e^{- x}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
